# Formatting rules for loz.
#
# Deliberately close to swiftformat's defaults so the diff stays small and the
# tool stays useful as a check rather than a constant source of churn.

--swiftversion 6.0

# 4-space indent, 96-column soft limit. The emulator has a lot of tabular code
# (opcode tables, palettes) that reads far better slightly wider than 80.
--indent 4
--maxwidth 96

# Keep aligned table literals and comment blocks intact — the opcode table and
# NES palette are laid out in grids on purpose, and wrapping them destroys the
# structure that makes them checkable by eye.
--wraparguments preserve
--wrapcollections preserve
--trimwhitespace nonblank-lines

# Explicit self only where required.
--self remove

# Rules deliberately disabled:
#
#   consecutiveSpaces  — the opcode table, NES palette, and APU period tables
#                        are laid out as grids so a shifted row is visible by
#                        eye. Collapsing runs of spaces destroys exactly the
#                        property that makes them reviewable.
#   wrapFunctionBodies — one-line accessors and helpers read better as one line.
#   blankLinesAroundMark — MARK sections here often follow a doc comment.
#   wrapIf/Loop/PropertyBodies — the CPU and PPU inner loops use compact
#                        single-line guards deliberately; expanding every one
#                        buries the logic in braces.
#   wrap, wrapArguments — these break the opcode table's 4-entries-per-row grid
#                        into ragged 3+1 lines. That grid is what makes a
#                        256-entry table verifiable by eye; a shifted row is
#                        immediately visible. Losing it to satisfy a column
#                        limit is a net loss of quality.
--disable consecutiveSpaces, wrapFunctionBodies, blankLinesAroundMark, \
          wrapIfStatementBodies, wrapLoopBodies, wrapPropertyBodies, \
          wrap, wrapArguments

--exclude .build,Reference

# Ranges without spaces: 0..<n reads better than 0 ..< n and matches
# prevailing Swift style.
--ranges nospace
